WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:08.496
This is Chapter Two, Episode Two: the Folketing, the Danish parliament.

3
00:00:09.046 --> 00:00:12.409
Picture the building on Slotsholmen in Copenhagen.

4
00:00:12.358 --> 00:00:18.996
The Folketing is Denmark's parliament, and together with the government, it holds the legislative power.

5
00:00:18.996 --> 00:00:22.002
It has its seat at Christiansborg in Copenhagen.

6
00:00:22.552 --> 00:00:24.177
Let's start with the people.

7
00:00:24.127 --> 00:00:27.652
The Folketing has 179 members.

8
00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:34.789
Of these, 175 are elected in Denmark, 2 in the Faroe Islands, and 2 in Greenland.

9
00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:45.660
At the latest general election, in 2026, 86 women were elected, which is 48 percent, and 93 men, which is 52 percent.

10
00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:55.023
Members of the Folketing almost always belong to a political party, and the political debate in the Folketing typically takes place between the parties.

11
00:00:54.972 --> 00:00:58.585
The individual members present their party's political views.

12
00:00:58.585 --> 00:01:02.297
The Folketing's meetings are, as a starting point, public.

13
00:01:02.297 --> 00:01:07.110
Anyone can come into the public gallery in the chamber and listen to the debates.

14
00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.310
This openness is an important part of democracy.

15
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:15.294
The meetings are also recorded, so you can watch them online, on the Folketing's website.

16
00:01:15.844 --> 00:01:17.431
Now to the calendar.

17
00:01:17.382 --> 00:01:23.719
The Folketing year begins on the first Tuesday of October, when the Folketing meets after the summer holiday.

18
00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:36.769
At that meeting, the prime minister gives a speech in which he or she gives an account of the state of the realm, that is, which challenges and opportunities the government sees, and what the government intends to do about them.

19
00:01:36.769 --> 00:01:40.031
This speech is called the prime minister's opening speech.

20
00:01:40.031 --> 00:01:43.152
The members of the Folketing then debate its content.

21
00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:49.639
When the Folketing handles a proposal for a new law, it happens in the chamber at Christiansborg.

22
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:54.752
Here the members from the political parties discuss every bill before they finally vote on it.

23
00:01:54.752 --> 00:02:00.552
Part of the legislative work also takes place in one of the many committees that the Folketing has set up.

24
00:02:00.552 --> 00:02:04.952
Each year the Folketing passes, on average, about 200 bills.

25
00:02:04.952 --> 00:02:11.730
One of them is the proposal for the finance act, the finanslov, which is the state's total budget for the next year.

26
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.793
The Folketing also decides who should have government power after an election.

27
00:02:16.743 --> 00:02:21.655
According to the constitution, it is formally the king who appoints the government.

28
00:02:21.655 --> 00:02:26.567
But the king must not appoint a government that has a majority in the Folketing against it.

29
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:29.316
That follows from the parliamentary principle.

30
00:02:29.866 --> 00:02:36.591
When a new government is to be formed, a so-called king's round, a kongerunde, is often held.

31
00:02:36.541 --> 00:02:48.303
In a king's round, two representatives of each party in turn go to the king to give advice on who they think should lead the negotiations on a new government, or who they think should be the new prime minister.

32
00:02:48.303 --> 00:03:02.046
Based on the advice the king receives, he can either appoint the person who has the most seats behind them as negotiation leader, or point directly to a person who gets the task of forming a new government with themself as prime minister.

33
00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:05.883
Now, how does the Folketing keep an eye on the government?

34
00:03:05.833 --> 00:03:10.721
It is the government and the public administration that must put the laws into effect.

35
00:03:10.721 --> 00:03:16.008
One of the Folketing's tasks is therefore to check that this happens the way the Folketing wanted.

36
00:03:16.008 --> 00:03:23.883
Control can, for example, be carried out by an individual member asking a minister to answer a question orally or in writing.

37
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:30.840
This is the most common way of exercising control, and several thousand questions are put to the ministers every year.

38
00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:36.940
One or more members can also raise an interpellation debate, a forespørgselsdebat.

39
00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.977
It allows for a somewhat longer debate in the chamber.

40
00:03:39.977 --> 00:03:47.427
The Folketing's standing, or permanent, committees can also call a minister in for a so-called consultation, a samråd.

41
00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:53.418
The minister must then appear before the committee and answer questions on concrete political subjects.

42
00:03:53.968 --> 00:04:02.843
In these ways, the Folketing can continuously follow the government's work and express its support for, or criticism of, the government.

43
00:04:02.793 --> 00:04:14.555
If a majority in the Folketing is, for example, very dissatisfied with a minister, the Folketing can force this minister to resign by expressing its lack of confidence in the minister.

44
00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:21.276
In practice, a minister often chooses to step down of their own accord if serious criticism is expressed.

45
00:04:21.826 --> 00:04:27.076
A majority in the Folketing can also express its lack of confidence in the whole government.

46
00:04:27.026 --> 00:04:32.688
That happens when a majority in the Folketing expresses its lack of confidence in the prime minister.

47
00:04:32.688 --> 00:04:36.626
It is called a motion of no confidence, a mistillidsvotum.

48
00:04:36.626 --> 00:04:41.790
The government must then resign, or the prime minister must call a new election.

49
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:49.727
During interpellation debates, the opposition often puts forward proposals that criticise the government's policy.

50
00:04:49.677 --> 00:04:55.677
However, proposals that amount to a vote of no confidence in the government are rarely adopted.

51
00:04:55.677 --> 00:05:02.790
But the possibility of doing so plays a significant role in the balance of power between the government and the opposition.

52
00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:08.477
The Folketing's power over the government and the ministers is the core of parliamentarism.

53
00:05:08.477 --> 00:05:13.656
The government and the individual ministers are thus politically accountable to the Folketing.

54
00:05:14.206 --> 00:05:27.210
And if a minister is suspected of having done something illegal in the course of their work as minister, the Folketing or the government can decide to bring them before a special court, the Court of Impeachment, Rigsretten.

55
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:36.660
Since Rigsretten was introduced with the first constitution in 1849, only six impeachment cases have been brought against ministers.

56
00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:45.697
Rigsretten consists of up to 15 judges from the Supreme Court, and just as many members who are elected by the Folketing for six years at a time.

57
00:05:45.697 --> 00:05:50.844
The members elected by the Folketing cannot at the same time be members of the Folketing.

58
00:05:51.394 --> 00:05:54.506
In recent times there have been two impeachment cases.

59
00:05:54.456 --> 00:06:08.819
One was in connection with the so-called Tamil case, where a former minister of justice in 1995 was given a four-month suspended prison sentence for having prevented Tamil refugees from Sri Lanka from getting family reunification.

60
00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:23.069
And in 2021, a former minister for immigration and integration was sentenced to 60 days of unconditional imprisonment for illegally separating asylum-seeking spouses and cohabiting couples at asylum centres.

61
00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:32.910
That former minister for immigration and integration was Inger Støjberg, who later founded the party Danmarksdemokraterne, which you met in Chapter One.

62
00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:36.460
Next: the workshops of the Folketing.

63
00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:43.797
A large part of the Folketing's work takes place in the standing, or permanent, committees, which are set up by the Folketing.

64
00:06:43.797 --> 00:06:46.510
They are often called the Folketing's workshops.

65
00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:54.048
In the chamber, the big lines are drawn up, but it is in the committees that politics is discussed thoroughly and in detail.

66
00:06:54.598 --> 00:06:57.723
The committees must deal with bills that are put forward.

67
00:06:57.673 --> 00:07:04.048
When a bill has had its first reading in the chamber, it is normally sent for closer consideration in a committee.

68
00:07:04.048 --> 00:07:11.310
Here, citizens or associations can, by agreement with the committee, come and present their views.

69
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:16.385
Everyone also has the opportunity to put forward their views by writing to the committee.

70
00:07:16.385 --> 00:07:21.698
When the committee has finished handling a bill, a report is written, a betænkning.

71
00:07:21.698 --> 00:07:26.535
It contains, among other things, the individual parties' positions on the bill.

72
00:07:26.535 --> 00:07:31.223
The standing committees also exercise control over the individual minister.

73
00:07:31.223 --> 00:07:38.874
A minister can, as mentioned, be called in for a consultation, and the committee's members can ask the minister questions.

74
00:07:39.424 --> 00:07:47.249
Each standing committee typically deals with the political matters that belong to a specific ministry's area of responsibility.

75
00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:59.361
The Folketing has, for example, a tax committee, a finance committee and an employment committee, because there is a tax ministry, a finance ministry and an employment ministry.

76
00:07:59.361 --> 00:08:11.532
The Folketing also has other standing committees, for example a European Affairs Committee, which deals with matters concerning cooperation in the EU, and which originate from all ministries.

77
00:08:12.082 --> 00:08:14.357
The number of committees varies.

78
00:08:14.307 --> 00:08:18.469
There are normally between 20 and 25 standing committees.

79
00:08:18.469 --> 00:08:22.607
The Folketing's committees consist only of members of the Folketing.

80
00:08:22.607 --> 00:08:27.994
A committee typically has 29 members, including a chair and a deputy chair.

81
00:08:27.994 --> 00:08:35.944
The committees are put together so that each party has a number of committee members that roughly corresponds to the party's size in the Folketing.

82
00:08:35.944 --> 00:08:41.094
The idea is that a majority in a committee should reflect a majority in the Folketing.

83
00:08:41.644 --> 00:08:48.644
According to the constitution, there must be a Foreign Policy Committee, Det Udenrigspolitiske Nævn.

84
00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:56.169
The government has a duty to consult the Foreign Policy Committee before it makes a decision of major foreign policy importance.

85
00:08:56.169 --> 00:09:10.560
That makes room for a broad political debate and broad political backing for the big foreign policy decisions, for example whether Denmark should use military force against another state, or enter into an important agreement with other countries.

86
00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:19.385
Because cooperation in the EU is so extensive, a European Affairs Committee, Europaudvalget, has also been set up.

87
00:09:19.335 --> 00:09:23.635
Its first and foremost task is to control Danish EU policy.

88
00:09:23.635 --> 00:09:31.835
In larger cases, the government must therefore ask the European Affairs Committee for advice before it goes to negotiations in the EU.

89
00:09:31.835 --> 00:09:36.635
On that basis, the committee gives the government a negotiating mandate.

90
00:09:36.635 --> 00:09:43.260
The mandate sets out Denmark's position on the matter, including whether the government can vote for or against a proposal.

91
00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:54.066
In this way, the European Affairs Committee gives the Folketing the opportunity to steer, for example, how the government votes at the meetings of the EU's Council of Ministers.

92
00:09:54.616 --> 00:09:56.791
Here is your recap for Episode Two.

93
00:09:56.741 --> 00:10:06.404
The Folketing has 179 members: 175 from Denmark, 2 from the Faroe Islands, and 2 from Greenland.

94
00:10:06.404 --> 00:10:08.453
It sits at Christiansborg.

95
00:10:08.453 --> 00:10:14.128
The Folketing year starts on the first Tuesday of October, with the prime minister's opening speech.

96
00:10:14.128 --> 00:10:18.191
About 200 bills are passed per year, including the finance act.

97
00:10:18.191 --> 00:10:24.816
The king formally appoints the government, after the king's round, but not against a majority in the Folketing.

98
00:10:24.816 --> 00:10:29.866
A motion of no confidence forces the government to resign or call an election.

99
00:10:29.866 --> 00:10:42.166
Rigsretten tries ministers: only six impeachment cases since 1849, with up to 15 Supreme Court judges and as many members elected by the Folketing for six years.

100
00:10:42.166 --> 00:10:47.541
There are normally 20 to 25 standing committees, typically with 29 members.

101
00:10:47.541 --> 00:10:57.972
The Foreign Policy Committee must be consulted on major foreign policy decisions, and the European Affairs Committee gives the government its negotiating mandate for the EU.

102
00:10:58.522 --> 00:11:01.122
Now it is time for your exam practice.

103
00:11:01.072 --> 00:11:05.985
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

104
00:11:05.985 --> 00:11:13.470
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

105
00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:15.270
Question 1.

106
00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:20.583
Can everyone, under normal circumstances, attend meetings in the Folketing chamber?

107
00:11:20.583 --> 00:11:22.208
Option A: Yes.

108
00:11:22.208 --> 00:11:23.856
Option B: No.
[DA] Kan alle under normale omstændigheder overvære møder i folketingssalen?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

109
00:11:30.406 --> 00:11:32.194
The answer is A: Yes.

110
00:11:32.144 --> 00:11:34.746
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

111
00:11:35.296 --> 00:11:36.546
Question 2.

112
00:11:36.496 --> 00:11:39.059
Who can be a member of a committee in the Folketing?

113
00:11:39.059 --> 00:11:42.809
Option A: Politicians who are members of the Folketing.

114
00:11:42.809 --> 00:11:46.308
Option B: Civil servants employed in a ministry.
[DA] Hvem kan være medlem af et udvalg i Folketinget?  A: Politikere, der er medlemmer af Folketinget  |  B: Embedsmænd, der er ansat i et ministerium

115
00:11:52.858 --> 00:11:56.908
The answer is A: Politicians who are members of the Folketing.

116
00:11:56.858 --> 00:11:59.478
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Politikere, der er medlemmer af Folketinget

117
00:11:59.928 --> 00:12:01.800
Question 3.

118
00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:10.464
Which court convicted the former minister for immigration and integration, Inger Støjberg, of illegally separating asylum-seeking couples?

119
00:12:10.464 --> 00:12:13.584
Option A: The High Court, Landsretten.

120
00:12:13.584 --> 00:12:17.448
Option B: The Supreme Court, Højesteret.

121
00:12:17.448 --> 00:12:21.264
Option C: The Court of Impeachment, Rigsretten.
[DA] Hvilken domstol dømte tidligere udlændinge- og integrationsminister Inger Støjberg for ulovligt at have adskilt asylsøgende par?  A: Landsretten  |  B: Højesteret  |  C: Rigsretten

122
00:12:27.814 --> 00:12:31.664
The answer is C: The Court of Impeachment, Rigsretten.

123
00:12:31.614 --> 00:12:34.410
It appeared in the June 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Rigsretten

124
00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:36.260
Question 4.

125
00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:43.710
What did the Folketing's indictment in the impeachment case against the former minister Inger Støjberg concern, among other things?

126
00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:48.210
Option A: Confiscation of refugees' valuables on entry.

127
00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:52.698
Option B: A temporary stop to family reunifications.

128
00:12:52.698 --> 00:12:57.924
Option C: Separation of asylum-seeking spouses and cohabiting couples.
[DA] Hvad omhandler Folketingets tiltale i rigsretssagen mod tidligere udlændinge- og integrationsmini- ster Inger Støjberg blandt andet?  A: Beslaglæggelse af flygtninges værdigenstande ved indrejse  |  B: Midlertidigt stop for familiesammenføringer  |  C: Adskillelse af asylsøgende ægtefæller og samlevende par

129
00:13:04.474 --> 00:13:09.924
The answer is C: Separation of asylum-seeking spouses and cohabiting couples.

130
00:13:09.874 --> 00:13:12.702
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Adskillelse af asylsøgende ægtefæller og samlevende par

131
00:13:13.252 --> 00:13:15.015
That's the end of this part.

132
00:13:14.965 --> 00:13:16.032
Well done.

